  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nWith high pressure situated overhead again today, another tranquil \nday is expected with mild and dry conditions. Near normal \ntemperatures are expected today with highs in the upper 70s to low \n80s as the airmass overhead moderates and light southwest winds \npersist. Comfortable humidity with dew points in the mid to upper \n50s continues today too. \n\nBecoming increasingly warmer and humid Wednesday with 40-70% chances \nfor rain and storms, especially by the afternoon and evening. \nScattered severe weather will be possible as SPC maintains a Slight \nRisk (level 2 of 5) for our entire forecast area. An upper level low \nwill deepen over the Hudson Bay and an attendant surface cold front \nis expected to move through during the afternoon and evening. \nScattered yet short-lived light showers may occur in the morning \naround daybreak with a leading weak shortwave, but they quickly \ndiminish. At least partial clearing should commence by the morning \ninto the early afternoon, allowing for the airmass overhead to \ndestabilize with increased WAA and low level moisture transport from \nveering southerly winds. Isolated discrete storms could develop as \nearly as 1-2 PM EDT in our far northwest near Lake Michigan \n(especially if there is a lake breeze to enhance lift), but still \nthinking the best chances for strong to severe storms hold off until \n3-4 PM EDT and later as the front moves east-southeast. Given the \nfavorable diurnal timing, highs in the low 80s, and dewpoints in the \nupper 60s to near 70, severe weather is possible as up to 2500 J/kg \nof surface based instability and decent 0-6 km shear of ~30 kts \nshould develop Wednesday afternoon and evening ahead of the front. \nThere is still some uncertainty to work out as to what exactly the \nstorm mode will be; it is possible that discrete supercells develop \ninitially before growing upscale into multicell/linear segments. All \nhazards will be possible including damaging wind gusts to 60 mph, \nquarter size hail, and a few tornadoes. It is worth noting that SPC \nhas a 5% tornado probability for northern Indiana and far southern \nLower Michigan when spin is maximized closest to the surface cold \nfront. Low level profiles on forecast soundings do appear favorable \nfor tornadoes with 100-150 m2/s2 0-1 SRH and LCLs of 750m, \nespecially if discrete supercells end up being the primary storm \nmode. The only ingredient that is lacking is the low and mid level \nlapse rates. If lapse rates of only 6 to 6.5 C/km occur tomorrow, \nthis may be a possible failure mode and/or limiting factor for \nconvection.\n\nAnother area of high pressure builds across the Midwest and Great \nLakes late in the week, bringing a return to dry conditions Thursday \nthrough Sunday. Have kept low chances (20-30%) for rain and storms \nfor Monday. Temperatures will remain near normal with a gradual \nwarming trend into the mid 80s for highs by the weekend into early \nnext week.",
